     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. A family visit and wanting things settled before rounds aren't clinical reasons for 50 mg. Amlodipine has a long half-life of about 30 to 50 hours, so a dose this size would keep acting for days and couldn't be reversed quickly. The risk is serious hypotension and shock in a patient who, as far as I can tell, has no unusual indication.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** For example, amlodipine 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily. If blood pressure is the concern, I can also add a note asking for titration at rounds.\n- **Enter the order with a note for pharmacy review.** If there is a clinical reason I don't know about, such as a specific protocol, please share it. Even then, I'd recommend pharmacist and attending sign-off before a dose above the labeled maximum.\n- **Look at faster options.** If the goal is faster blood pressure control, other agents are better suited to that than a very large dose of a slow-acting drug. That choice is yours and the team's to make.\n\nTell me which you'd like, and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
